## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:   
Polymers of intrinsic microporosity   
Zwitterion   
Lithium-sulfur battery
Functional separator which facilitates the fast transport of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ but obstructs the permeation of polysulfides is highly required for durable lithium-sulfur (Li-S) batteries. Here, we report a facile approach to developing such functional separator by coating the commercial polypropylene separator with a unique solution-processable polymer featuring both intrinsic microporosity and ion-selective functionality. The polymer is designed with contorted and rigid backbones, which incorporates zwitterionic side chains with both sulfophilic and lithiophilic groups, to produce confined ionic channels for selective ion transport via the chemical interactions with lithium polysulfides. Therefore, when used in the cell configuration, the as-developed functional separator can not only allow the fast ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ conduction but also obstruct the penetration of bulky polysulfides, thus resulting in obvious shuttle suppression as well as the resultant credible and stable sulfur electrochemistry. Thanks to these advan­ tages, the Li-S batteries implementing the as-developed functional separator possess high cycling ability and decent rate performance.
## 1. Introduction
Lithium-sulfur (Li-S) batteries are proposed to be one of the most prospective substitutes of conventional lithium-ion batteries for nextgeneration electrochemical energy storage systems [1–3]. However, despite the charming merits of high theoretical energy density, envi­ ronmental benignity, non-toxicity, and low cost of sulfur, the achieve­ ment of credible and stable sulfur electrochemistry is still complicated by some tricky technical challenges that bring about unacceptable ca­ pacity and cycling life [4,5]. Among them, the “shuttle effect” represents the most vital limitation that restricts the commercial application of Li-S batteries [6]. Owing to their high dissolution and flooding diffusion in the liquid-state electrolyte, the intermediate lithium polysulfides (LiPS) carry out parasitic shuttling behavior between the cathode and anode during the charge-discharge process, resulting in irreversible sulfur loss, terrible electrode corrosion, and low Coulombic efficiency.
Given that shuttle effect results from a combination of dissolution and diffusion of polysulfides, a technique allowing the dissolution but blocking the diffusion can be a rational option to address this issue [7,8]. Based on such concept, the development of a functional separator has become one of the most relevant tactics [9–11]. The basic principle underlying this tactic lies in the interactions between polysulfides and functional materials on the cathodic side of the separator, which can be further categorized into two groups, namely, anchoring mechanism and rejecting mechanism [12]. The former exploits various adsorbents such as metal oxides/sulfides, porous carbon materials, metal organic frameworks (MOFs), and microporous organic polymers (MOPs) to physically or chemically trap and reuse sulfur species, thus realizing effective improvement of sulfur utilization and inhibition of polysulfide shuttling at the same time [4,13–16]. However, this type of functional separator is usually subjected to the limitation of porous structure and adsorption sites, which leads to a reduced polysulfide-impeding effect especially under a high sulfur-loading condition [12]. Regarding the latter, the functional separator is designed to electrostatically reject polysulfide anions and confine them within the cathode compartment, which can be fabricated by coating commercial separator with ion-selective polymers such as Nafion and poly(acrylic acid) [17–20]. This design provides good sulfur-restraining capability owing to the ion selective nature, but still suffers from slow ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ transfer in the dense and non-porous polymer layer and the consequential degeneration of redox kinetics [21]. Therefore, the development of an advanced ion-selective polymer that can simultaneously achieve effective polysulfide block­ ing and good ${\bf L i}^{+}$ conduction is essential for the pursuit of credible solution-based sulfur electrochemistry.
The latest advance of polymers of intrinsic microporosity (PIMs) offers a novel platform for the design of ion-selective polymers with molecularly adjustable pore architecture that facilitate rapid and se­ lective ion transfer [22–27]. For instance, Zuo et al. reported a series of sulfonated microporous polymers with rigid and contorted backbones that enable fast and highly selective transport of cations [24]. Moreover, owing to their rigid backbones, ion-selective microporous polymers generally exhibit enhanced ionic conductivity and high stability [23]. As such, it is reasonable to believe that ion-selective polymers derived from PIMs have a great potential for effective polysulfide suppression and fast ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ conduction. Nevertheless, as far as we know, ion-selective micro­ porous polymers have not been studied for this application.
Herein, we synthesize a novel zwitterionic microporous polymer (QTB-SA) through a facile post-synthetic modification of a Tr¨oger’s base containing PIM (TB), which is then used to modify the commercial polypropylene (PP) separator for effective polysulfide suppression. Because zwitterionic side chain comprising one sulfonate group and one quaternary ammonium is tethered, the nano-sized cavities and confined channels within QTB-SA are functionalized with both lithiophilic and sulfophilic groups. Because sulfonate terminals preferably intimate ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ while quaternary ammonium groups can act as electron acceptor to anchor the polysulfide anions, this distinct microporous zwitterion conduces to the improvement of LiPS dissociation, restriction on poly­ sulfide diffusion, and facilitation to $\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ transport [21]. Thanks to these advantages, batteries based upon QTB-SA modified separator can ach­ ieve a decent rate capacity of $845.8\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $2\mathsf{C}$ as well as long cycle performance at 1 C (capacity fading rate of $0.042\%$ per cycle upon 500 cycles). These encouraging results show that the as-obtained QTB-SA has a huge advantage in the effective shuttle suppression, providing a new approach to increasing the reliability of sulfur electrochemistry as well as boosting the performance of Li-S batteries.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Diamino- $^{\cdot3,3^{\prime}}$ -dimethylbiphenyl, trifluoroacetic acid, dime­ thoxymethane, 1,3-propanesulfonate, ammonia aqueous solution $(25-28\%)$ ), 1,2-dimethoxyethane (DME), 1,3-dioxolane (DOL), lith­ iumbis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI), multi-wall carbon nanotubes (MWCNT), $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{.}$ , and elemental sulfur were obtained from Aladdin Chemistry Co., Ltd. Super P carbon and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) were purchased from Alfa Aesar. Methanol, ethanol, dichloro­ methane and N-methyl pyrrolidone (NMP) were supplied by Anhui Zesheng Technology Co., Ltd. All chemicals were used directly without any further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Tro¨ger’s base polymer (TB)
Tr¨oger’s base polymer (TB) was prepared following a previously reported procedure [23]. As shown in Scheme 1, a mixture of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dia­ mino- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -dimethylbiphenyl $\left(5\mathrm{\textrm{g}},\ 23.5\mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ in dimethoxymethane $21\mathrm{mL},$ $117.9\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{\cdot}$ was cooled to $0^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and trifluoroacetic acid $\mathrm{\Omega}^{(40\mathrm{mL})}$ was added slowly. The mixture was stirred at room temperature for $96\mathrm{h}$ and then poured slowly into vigorously stirred ammonia aqueous solu­ tion. The precipitate was filtered, washed with excess deionized water, and purified by dissolving in chloroform and reprecipitating in meth­ anol. The light-yellow colored polymer fibers were finally collected by filtration, and dried under vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to give TB.
## 2.3. Synthesis of Tro¨ger’s base polymer containing zwitterionic side chain (QTB-SA-x)
QTB-SA- $x$ was synthesized by a simple reaction between TB and a certain amount of 1,3-propanesulfonate. The degree of functionalization (the number of zwitterionic ion/repeat unit, $x$ value) of QTB-SA- $x$ could be controlled by adjusting the addition amount of 1,3-propanesulfonate. A typical synthesis procedure of QTB-SA-0.56 was shown as follows: A mixture of TB (5 mmol, $1.241\mathrm{g}$ ), 1,3-propanesulfonate (3 mmol, 0.3664 g), and NMP $\mathrm{15{mL}}$ was charged into a Pyrex tube and stirred at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the mixture was added slowly into excess ethyl acetate. The solid product was collected by filtration, thoroughly washed with ethyl acetate and methanol, and dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ in vacuo to give QTB-SA-0.56.
## 2.4. Separator fabrication
QTB-SA-0.56 was dissolved in NMP at a concentration of $0.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . This solution was filtered through a polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) springe filter $(0.45~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ pore size) and coated on a commercial PP sepa­ rator (Celgard 2500). After annealed in an oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ the QTB-SA modified separator was obtained. For reference purpose, the TB coated separator was prepared using an identical procedure.
## 2.5. Preparation of sulfur electrode
Sulfur/MWCNT composite was firstly prepared by mixing elemental sulfur and commercial MWCNT at a weight ratio of 3:1 and then heating at $155^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under argon atmosphere. The sulfur content in the resultant composite was determined by TGA measurement. The sulfur electrode was fabricated by casting the slurry consisting of S/MWCNT, Super P, and PVDF at weight ratio of 7:2:1 onto an aluminum foil and drying in an oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{{h}}}$ . The sulfur loading was controlled at $0.75~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ .
## 2.6. Characterization methods
## 2.7. Electrochemical characterization
The Li-S coin type (CR2032) cell was assembled in an argon-filled glove box with sulfur cathode, different separators, and lithium anode for electrochemical evaluation. The diameter size of cathode, separator and anode is $10\ \mathrm{mm}$ , $19~\mathrm{mm}$ and $16~\mathrm{mm}$ , respectively. The electrolyte was composed of $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ lithium bis(trifluoromethane sulfonyl) imide (LiTFSI) dissolved in binary solvent of DOL and DME (1:1 in volume) containing $1\mathrm{wt}\%\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ . The electrolyte addition was fixed at $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ per $1~\mathrm{mg}$ sulfur. Galvanostatic charge-discharge cycling and rate perfor­ mance were studied using a Neware battery cycler within a voltage window of $1.7{-}2.8\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ ). The capacities and current densities were calculated based on the mass of sulfur content in the cathode (1 C $=1675\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1};$ ). Cyclic voltammetry (CV) was acquired by using CorrTest electrochemical workstation in the voltage range from 1.7 to $2.8\mathrm{V}$ (vs. $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}.$ ). Lithium ion diffusion coefficient $(D_{\mathrm{Li+}})$ was evaluated by CV curves and calculated according to the Randles-Sevick equation [28].
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was obtained by CorrTest electrochemical workstation in the frequency range from 0.01 Hz to $10^{3}~\mathrm{kHz}$ at open-circuit voltage. The ionic conductivity (σ) was measured by EIS spectra in the stainless steel (SS) | separator | SS symmetric configuration according to the following equation:
$$
\sigma=\frac{L}{R_{b}\ \times A}
$$
where $\sigma$ was the ionic conductivity $\mathrm{{(mS\thinspacecm^{-1}}}.$ ), $L$ stood for the thickness of the membrane (cm), $R_{\mathrm{b}}$ represented the bulk resistance $\left(\Omega\right)$ , and A was the surface area of the SS electrode $(\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ . ${\bf L i}^{+}$ transference number $(t_{\mathrm{Li+}})$ was measured in a Li | separator | Li symmetric configuration by using CHI660D electrochemical workstation at a constant step potential of 10 $\mathrm{mV}$ and calculated from the equation as follows [14,29]:
$$
\mathfrak{t}_{\mathrm{Li}+}=\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{s}}/\mathrm{I}_{0}
$$
where $t_{L i+}$ was $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ transference number, while $I_{s}$ and $I_{O}$ represented the current at the steady state and initial state, respectively. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) measurement was performed in a three-electrode configuration with 0.1 M Li2S/methanol and $\mathtt{A g/A g C l}$ electrode as electrolyte and a reference electrode, respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterization of polymers
As shown in Scheme 1, TB was synthesized from $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -diamino- $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ - dimethylbiphenyl and dimethoxymethane following the procedure previously reported [23]. Subsequently, zwitterionic group-modified TB (QTB-SA- $x_{\cdot}$ ) was prepared using a controlled reaction of the tertiary amine groups in TB with 1,3-propanesultone. The resultant QTB-SA polymer was insoluble in THF and $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ , but soluble in NMP and DMSO. This solubility preference was contrary to what was observed for TB, implying that the Tr¨oger’s base units on the backbones of QTB-SA were ionized. The successful incorporation of zwitterionic side chains into QTB-SA was further verified by FT-IR and $^1\mathrm{H}\mathrm{NMR}$ . As displayed in Fig. 1a, the new bands relating to the stretching vibrations of sulfonate groups at around 1235 and $1032\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were observed in FT-IR spectrum of QTB-SA polymer. In addition, a new peak assigned to alkyl-chain protons emerged at 1.7 ppm in $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum of QTB-SA, confirm­ ing the successful introduction of zwitterionic side chain (Fig. 1b).
The DF value of QTB-SA- $x$ , where DF represented the degree of functionalization (the number of zwitterionic ion/repeat unit, $x$ value), was determined by elemental analysis. As shown in Table S1, DF value of 0.56 and 0.83 was observed for QTB-SA- $x$ synthesized at a 1,3-propane­ sultone/TB molar ratio of 0.6:1 and 1:1, respectively. Obviously, a higher DF value was observed for sample prepared by adding a higher amount of 1,3-propanesultone. Moreover, the ion exchange capacity (IEC) of QTB-SA- $x$ could be also evaluated by elemental analysis data. Again, a higher IEC was observed for sample developed from higher content of 1,3-propanesultone. As such, the DF and IEC of QTB-SA- $x$ could be readily tuned by simply adjusting the amount of 1,3-propane­ sultone used. Nevertheless, for simplicity, only one representative polymer, i.e., QTB-SA-0.56 (labelled as QTB-SA in the following sec­ tions) was used to evaluate the advantages of microporous zwitterion in adjusting the sulfur electrochemistry within Li-S battery.
The thermal stability of polymers was estimated using thermogra­ vimetric analysis (TGA) in a nitrogen atmosphere (Fig. S1). Two decomposition steps were observed for QTB-SA. The first decomposition step, at a temperature range from 180 to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , was ascribed to the loss of zwitterionic side chain, whereas the second step corresponded to the degradation of TB backbone. The porosity properties of the as-prepared polymers were investigated by nitrogen sorption isotherms tested at 77 K (Fig. 1c). Similar to other porous polymers with pre- or postsynthetically introduced polar functionalities, the BET surface area of QTB-SA $(67\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ was markedly smaller than that of TB $(339\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ [23,30–32]. This was presumably owing to the partial space occupation and pore blocking by the bulky zwitterionic side chain. The pore size distribution calculated by NLDFT method verified that both TB and QTB-SA were predominated microporous, with pore size centered at around $1.4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 1d).
## 3.2. Fabrication and characterization of QTB-SA-modified separato
QTB-SA was coated on one side of the commercial PP membrane to prepare the modified separator of QTB-SA/PP. For comparison pur­ poses, TB modified separator (TB/PP) was formed using an identical procedure. SEM was used to study the surface and cross-sectional mor­ phologies of the separators. As shown in Fig. 2a, the pristine PP sepa­ rator possessed a porous structure with micrometer-sized pores. In contrast, QTB-SA- and TB-modified separators showed a uniform poly­ mer coating with complete filling of macropores (Fig. 2b and S2a). The cross-sectional SEM images reveal ${\sim}1.1{-}1.3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thick uniform coating of polymers on the separators (Fig. 2c and S2b). Compared with the mac­ ropores of the pristine PP separator, the micropores in QTB-SA and TB layers are more favorable to selective transport of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and obstructing bulky polysulfides.
The surface wettability of the resultant separators was investigated by contact angle measurements. In comparison with PP separator, the existence of QTB-SA coating layer improved the wettability to electro­ lyte, with contact angle decreasing from $34.8^{\circ}$ for PP to $19.5^{\circ}$ for QTBSA/PP separator (Fig. S3). This indicated the superior affinity of QTB-SA for electrolyte, which was beneficial to electrolyte penetration and $\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ migration. The significant improvement in wettability of QTB-SA/PP separator was mainly owing to the zwitterionic groups located on the coating layer, which would absorb more polar solvent molecules to form big clusters in the polymer domains and in turn enhance the wettability of the separator [18]. As for TB polymer having no ionic groups, its affinity to electrolyte was much poorer than that of QTB-SA. Never­ theless, TB/PP had a contact angle of $30.1^{\circ}$ for electrolyte drop, with the surface wettability property slight better than that of pristine PP
