membrane.
To evaluate the polysulfide regulation, a visual demonstration of polysulfides diffusion was performed on the QTB-SA/PP membrane, where $0.2\mathrm{~M~}$ LiPS $\mathrm{(Li_{2}S_{4})}$ solution and blank DME/DOL solvent were partitioned by pristine PP membrane or polymer-modified ones in a Hshaped cell (Fig. 2d). As expected, when pristine PP separator was used, the opposite chamber changed from colorless to yellow after $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ and further to dark yellow after $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ , indicating a considerable polysulfide crossover. In contrast, only a light-yellow solution was observed for the TB modified separator after $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ of the diffusion experiment, indicating an improved blocking of the polysulfide diffusion. Particularly, the assynthesized QTB-SA/PP separator was able to maintain colorless even after $12\mathrm{~h~}$ , demonstrating its strong ability to block polysulfide cross­ over. Such superior polysulfide blockage was presumably resulted from the synergistic effect of physical adsorption from micropores and strong electrostatic interaction between zwitterionic ions and LiPS, which immobilized LiPS and provided an effective barrier to prevent pene­ tration of polysulfides. This favorable characteristic was expected to significantly minimize polysulfide shuttle and stabilize the chemistry properties of the corresponding Li-S batteries.
The interactions between QTB-SA or TB and LiPS were examined by XPS measurements. After adsorbing $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}.$ , the resultant composites were denoted as $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}@\mathrm{QTB}{\cdot}S\mathrm{A}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}@\mathrm{TB}$ . As illustrated in Fig. 2e, the Li 1s spectra of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}@\mathrm{TB}$ , and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}@\mathrm{QTB}{\cdot}S\mathrm{A}$ showed a consistent peak at $55.7\mathrm{eV}$ , which could be attributed to the Li-S bonding. However, a new peak related to Li-O bonding emerged at $56.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the Li 1s spectrum of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}@\mathrm{QTB}{\cdot}S\mathrm{A}$ , confirming the interaction between $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and - $.s{\mathrm{O}}_{3}{\cdot}$ . As shown in Fig. 2f, the N 1s spectrum of QTB-SA showed two peaks at 399.2 and $400.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which were attributed to tertiary amine N and quaternary ammonium N, respectively. Notably, the latter peak moved towards lower binding energy for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}@\mathrm{QTB}{\cdot}S\mathrm{A}$ , demonstrating the coordination of quaternary ammonium with polysulfide anion. Regarding $\textsf{s}2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum, every kind of S had two different peaks ascribed to $\mathsf{s}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and S $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ [9]. QTB-SA could be resolved into one doublet positioned at 167.8 and $168.9\mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to sulfonate S, while $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{4}}$ showed four peaks assigned to the terminal S $({S_{\mathrm{T}}}^{-1})$ and bridging S $({\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{B}}}^{0})$ as marked at 161.4 and $163.2\mathrm{eV}$ in Fig. 2g. It should be noted that, compared to the binding energy of these pure materials, there were positive binding energy shifts for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}@{\sf Q T B-S A},$ indicating the decreased electron cloud density in sulfur. This evidence suggested the coordination between polysulfides and quaternary ammonium as well as sulfonate and $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ . As a consequence, the synergistic effect of Li-O and S-N enabled the QTB-SA to effectively trap LiPS.
Besides polysulfide obstruction, the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ conductivity is another cri­ terion for reasonable separator fabrication in Li-S cells, which was illustrated by comparing the $\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ transference number $({t_{\mathrm{Li}}}^{+})$ and ionic conductivity between different separators. As shown in Fig. S4, the ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ transference number in the QTB-SA/PP separator (0.908) is higher than the TB/PP separator (0.830) and pristine PP separator (0.886), indi­ cating its good ${\bf L i}^{+}$ transfer performance. Additionally, the ionic migration resistance and conductivity of separators were evaluated by carried out electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests. As shown in Fig. S5, the TB/PP separator exhibited a larger resistance (4.28 $\Omega_{\mathcal{\Lambda}}^{\setminus}$ ) than pristine PP separator (1.01 Ω). Nevertheless, the QTB-SA/PP reduced the resistance to a comparatively small value of $1.21\ \Omega$ . As a result, QTB-SA/PP and pristine PP delivered almost the same level of ion conductivity (0.73 and $0.87~\mathrm{m}S~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively), which was much higher than that of TB/PP $(0.21~\mathrm{m}S~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}).$ ). These results demonstrated that QTB-SA/PP separator had an excellent capability for ${\bf L i}^{+}$ selective conduction. The high $\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ conduction of QTB-SA/PP in comparison with TB/PP would be ascribed to the introduction of negative charged sul­ fonate groups in the former, which facilitated the transport of positively charged ${\mathbf{{Li}}^{+}}$ ions.
Overall, the above results confirmed that the QTB-SA coating layer could effectively block bulky polysulfides while enabling fast ${\bf L i}^{+}$ transport due to a combination of electrostatic interaction and repulsion and size exclusion. Specifically, as illustrated in Fig. 2h, due to the rigid and contorted backbone, our zwitterionic microporous polymer formed nanoconfined inter-connected ionic channels. The electrostatic interac­ tion between polysulfides and quaternary ammonium groups not only sharply reduced the pore size of micropores but also formed a polysulfide-concentrated shield, which strongly obstructed the further penetration of polysulfides. Meanwhile, the microporosity and interac­ tion with sulfonate groups facilitated the fast transport of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ within the QTB-SA barrier.
## 3.3. Electrochemistry of QTB-SA/PP-based Li-S batteries
The as-constructed QTB-SA/PP separator was studied with CR2032 type coin cells to evaluate its effect on the electrochemical behaviors of Li-S batteries. Fig. 3a displayed the galvanostatic charge−discharge profiles of the cells implementing QTB-SA/PP separator at 0.2C and at different cycle numbers (1st, 10th, $50{\mathrm{th}}_{;}$ , and 100th cycles). Typical dual-plateau discharge curves could be observed at around 2.35 and $2.11~\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , which represented the reduction of elemental sulfur to highorder soluble LiPS $(\mathrm{Li_{2}S_{n}},$ $4\leq\mathtt{n}\leq8\$ ) and their further conversation into insoluble $\mathrm{Li_{2}S_{2}}/\mathrm{Li_{2}}S$ . In the charging profiles, one charging slope was observed and assigned to the oxidation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ to elemental sulfur. Notably, the voltage profile remained well after 100 cycles with ignorable polarization exacerbation, implying fast redox kinetics and stable sulfur electrochemistry. By contrast, the Li-S cell with the pristine PP separator showed a dramatic capacity fading and serious polarization during the cycling process, while that with TB coated separator also revealed a relatively severe capacity fading and polarization compared to that with QTB-SA/PP separator (Fig. S6). To further explore the electrochemical property of the Li-S cell based on QTB-SA/PP separator, the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curve was tested and shown in Fig. 3b. Consistent with the voltage profiles, the typical two cathodic peaks and one overlapped anodic peak were observed. The position and current of the redox peaks remained well uncharged in the initial 3 cycles, indi­ cating good electrochemical reversibility.
The stability of the cells implementing different separators was evaluated by cycling performance test. As displayed in Fig. 3c, the cell based on pristine PP separator possessed a poor capacity retention rate of $66.8\%$ after 100 cycles at 0.2C. After the introduction of TB coating layer, a slight improvement of cycling stability occurred, showing ca­ pacity retention rate of $76.9\%$ . Interestingly, a more significant increase of cycling stability was observed on QTB-SA/PP-based cell, with ca­ pacity retention rate further increasing to $82.9\%$ . Moreover, under a higher sulfur loading $(1.8\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ), the capacity retention of QTB-SA/ PP-based cell after 100 cycles at 0.2C was $70.3\%$ , much higher than that of TB/PP- $(59.7\%)$ and virgin PP-based cells $(58.8\%)$ (Fig. S7). These are owing to the synergistic effect of electrostatic interaction between LiPS and zwitterionic groups on improving shielding effect of QTB-SA coating layer. As a consequence, the as-constructed QTB-SA/PP could serve as an extremely efficient polysulfide barrier to prevent their diffusion, thereby restricting the sulfur species in the cathode to be effectively employed rather than continuously losing by the shuttle effect.
The rate capability of the cells implementing different separators was also explored and illustrated in Fig. 3d. The capacity of PP- and TB/PPbased batteries decreased severely as current rate increased, leading to a capacity of 707.3 and $720.6\operatorname{mAh}{\ g^{-1}}$ at $2\mathsf{C}$ , respectively. In comparison, the QTB-SA/PP cell had a greatly superior rate performance, with a higher capacity of $845.8\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathsf{2C},$ which restored to $1071.3\mathrm{mAh}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ when the current was switched back to 0.2C, demonstrating a high degree of reversibility and accelerated sulfur redox reactions. Besides, a long-term cycling performance was carried out at 1 C and displayed in Fig. 3e. As for QTB-SA/PP-based cell, a favorable capacity retention of $722.3\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was remained after 500 cycles with the average capacity attenuation of $0.042\%$ per cycle. The above results demonstrated the great advantage of zwitterionic QTB-SA in promoting and stabilizing sulfur electrochemistry for advanced Li S cells. Moreover, as shown in
Table S2, the performance of our QTB-SA/PP-based cell is one of the best in the literatures with PIMs-coated PP separators, further demonstrating the advantages of our zwitterionic QTB-SA polymer for advanced Li-S cells [15,33,34].
The greatly improved performances of QTB-SA/PP based cells including high reversible capacity, good rate performance, and good long-term cyclability were ascribed to the effective suppression of pol­ ysulfide shuttling and promoted redox kinetics with the QTB-SA barrier layer. The effective shuttle inhibition of QTB-SA barrier was further examined by recording the self-discharging behavior of cells with different separators. As illustrated in Fig. 4a, the open-circuit voltage (OCV) of QTB-SA/PP cell was much steadier and higher in comparison with TB/PP and PP counterparts, which consistently demonstrating that the as-constructed QTB-SA barrier had the capability to obstruct poly­ sulfide shuttling and stabilize sulfur electrochemistry. To prove the ki­ netic improvement by the QTB-SA barrier, the Nyquist plots of the three cells were calculated from EIS as shown in Fig. 4b. It is well known that the semicircle at high frequency responses to charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ . The cells using QTB-SA/PP and PP separator displayed a similar $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value, probably owing to the ultrathin thickness of QTB-SA coating layer. Nevertheless, the cell with TB/PP separator showed much larger $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ than that with PP separator. In consideration of the nearly identical thickness of QTB-SA and TB coating layers, the increase in charge transfer kinetics during the polysulfide conversion process could only be attributed to zwitterionic nature of the QTB-SA, which improved LiPS dissociation and facilitated $\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ transport. Furthermore, the ${\bf L i}^{+}$ diffusion coefficient $(D_{\mathrm{Li+}})$ of the separators were also calculated from CV results.
As illustrated in Fig. S8, the QTB-SA/PP delivered higher slope than that of TB/PP for all the peaks in the CV profiles, suggesting the significantly higher $D_{\mathrm{Li+}}$ of QTB-SA/PP separator. We believed the negative sulfonate sites in the micropores of QTB-SA were favorable for rapid ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ con­ duction [24].
Additionally, owing to its strong interaction with LiPS together with good charge transfer characteristics, the as-constructed QTB-SA could also function as a promising electrocatalyst to promote electrochemistry of sulfur. To demonstrate the catalytic performance of QTB-SA in Li-S chemistry, the redox potential in the CV curves of symmetric cells was measured. As displayed in Fig. 4c, the CV curve of the QTB-SA sym­ metrical cell had two pairs of high-current redox peaks $(0.55/-0.55$ and $0.84/{-0.84}\mathrm{~V~}$ , respectively), demonstrating its great capability of cata­ lyzing the LiPS conversion attributed to the zwitterion structure of QTBSA [21,35]. In contrast, the electrode derived from TB exhibited a pair of broad peaks with reduced current density. As a result, the zwitterionic ion played a significant role in enhancing the catalytic performance of QTB-SA. Apart from that, the catalytic activity of QTB-SA toward LiPS conversion was examined by the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV). As shown in Fig. 4d, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ oxidation underwent a sluggish kinetics on glassy carbon with negligible current response even under a relatively large overpotential. In contrast, both QTB-SA and TB possessed a certain extent of kinetic improvement with more sensitive current responses. In particular, the QTB-SA displayed a lower onset potential (at about -0.37 V) and higher oxidation current compared with TB, evidencing its better effectiveness in lowering the activation energy of the sulfur conversion from solid $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ to soluble LiPS [9,36]. In view of the above results, the strong chemical interactions between LiPS and zwitterionic ions of QTB-SA exerted an efficient catalytic effect on the sulfur conversion reactions, thereby significantly enhancing the cycling stability and rate capability of the corresponding Li-S batteries.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, we proposed a novel strategy for the development of functionalized polymer of intrinsic microporosity for effective poly­ sulfide obstruction toward highly durable Li-S batteries, by exploiting polymer with zwitterion groups and rigid contorted backbones that enabled fast and selective ion transport. The unique structure of the asdeveloped polymer (QTB-SA) provided the following advantages: i) The regular micropores were beneficial to supply channels for $\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ conduc­ tion but inhibit the diffusion of polysulfides; ii) The zwitterion groups offered lithiopholic and sulfophilic sites in the micropores, empowering facile ${\bf L i}^{+}$ conduction and strong polysulfide immobilization simulta­ neously; iii) The strong chemical interactions between LiPS and zwit­ terionic ions of QTB-SA exerted an efficient catalytic effect on the sulfur conversion reactions. Benefiting from these advantages, significantly enhanced cycling stability and rate performance could be achieved for the Li-S batteries implementing QTB-SA modified separator. This work opened up a new way for reasonable polysulfide regulation and had broad prospects of boosting the future development of practical Li-S batteries.
